d "b6aac796-5f94-58f0-b828-88b8f756ca51" :question/math-academy-id "q-22826" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & >0 \\\\ 2 & >\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that $x>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-22826/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-22826"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-343133/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "3d50be51-265e-5b4d-8db4-b7fe0b52fe02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343133/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "87138f5c-d35f-53d1-b15d-bbe546195b5d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-343133/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-343133/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e