 :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344779/field-4/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-344779/field-4/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344779" :question/id #uuid "1f2a740b-f188-558e-8ff6-b981320a0982" :question/math-academy-id "q-344779" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083206/assets/a554b63796bbf63daa8fafccb26cec460ec3bf871c4c6c751ee07b0c343d5105.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-3,$ $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $x=3,$ and $x=5,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave down are\n\n$(-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$),\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$),\\,(${{field-4}}$,5).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-1,1),$ and $(3,5).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave down on the intervals \n\n$$\n(\\,-\\infty ,-3\\,),\\,(\\,-1,1\\,),\\,(\\,3,5\\,).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344779/field-1" "q-344779/field-2" "q-344779/field-3" "q-344779/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344779"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
